  - "Experience: player characters start at level one (NPCs 1D4+3 or as set by the G.M.). The entry says to use the Birdman experience table, and the Experience Tables page heads that ladder Birdman & Junk Crab."
  - "Magic: the four listed spells, plus one Spell Invocation of level one or two at levels 3, 6, 9 and 12. Cannot repair or modify Techno-Wizard or other magic items."
  - "Psionics: Object Read, Telemechanics and Telemechanic: Mental Operation, plus one Physical power at levels 1, 5, 8, 11 and 15. The entry does not say whether the race counts as a minor, major or master psychic; it is stored as major, a judgement, so use the tier the G.M. rules for the save vs psionics."
  - "Money and equipment: none printed. Junk Crabs do not understand money or credits and work for trade in scrap, salvage, E-Clips and bits of technology. Everything they own is the shell and what is built into it."
  - "Horror Factor: not applicable; they often look comical."
  - "Cannot handle really high-tech equipment or Techno-Wizard items, and has no gift for spoken language: the native speech is clicks, barks and hums, effectively the same as that of the Milu, and human languages come out in short phrases. The R.C.C. skill list grants no language skill."
side_effects: "Seen outside the junk shell a Junk Crab is -5 to M.A. and -3 to M.E. and P.B., is shy and skittish, and loses the confidence to do any mechanical work except building a new shell. More than four days out of water is crippling and then fatal (see Amphibious Being). As slaves they are useless: they give up and die in captivity."
extraction_notes: "Lemuria printed 43-47 (cache p044-p048), under Aquatic Races; the heading is Junk Crab R.C.C. and the entry ends at Value on printed 47, above the Lemurian R.C.C. heading. Every figure was read off 150 dpi renders of the five pages; the Experience Tables (printed 86) were read off a render only. A race, so men_of_arms is not stated: mdc_base carries the pool. || XP: ladder headed Birdman & Junk Crab on printed 86, bands 0-2,100 / 2,101-4,200 / 4,201-8,400 / 8,401-17,250 / 17,251-25,500 / 25,501-35,900 / 35,901-51,000 / 51,001-71,000 / 71,001-96,000 / 96,001-131,700 / 131,701-181,900 / 181,901-232,200 / 232,201-282,400 / 282,401-342,500 / 342,501-403,000; it must stay identical to the Birdman Warrior''s. || POOLS: Main Body (without shell) P.E. x2 +1D6 per level stored as mdc_base; the other locations and the shell are ability text. P.P.E. 1D6x10 + P.E. +1D6+1 per level, printed twice (P.P.E. line and Magic line) and the same both times. I.S.P. M.E. x3 +1D6+3 per level. || PRINTED DISAGREEMENTS, both kept in prose and neither resolved: shell M.D.C. 5D6x10+35 (ability 3, printed 44) against 4D6x10+35 (M.D.C. by Location, printed 46); shell height 10-15 feet (introduction, printed 43) against up to 8 feet (Size, printed 46). Fixed Position Ion Weapon prints 1,000 feet (610 m); the feet figure was kept. Underwater Hover Jet prints long C-Clip, read as E-Clip. || BONUSES stored: +4 Perception, +2 initiative, +1 strike, parry and dodge, +1 disarm, +3 pull punch, +4 roll with impact, +2 save vs disease; Attacks per Melee Three stored as attacks_base 3. Prose only, as conditional: six attacks when technology is being used, Perception +6 in the named cases, +3 dodge and Prowl 64% and double Spd outside the shell, -5 M.A. and -3 M.E. and P.B. seen outside the shell, the Sensor Pod and flight bonuses. || SKILLS: catalog base plus the printed bonus. Printed Pilot: Water Scooters is the catalog''s Water Scooters; Water Skiing and Surfing is Water Skiing & Surfing; General Repair and Maintenance is General Repair & Maintenance; printed Recycle is the catalog row Recycle (Domestic), not Recycling. Salvage is printed (20%) with no plus sign and was read as +20%. Sensory Equipment or T.V./Video (+10%) is a choose-1. Two W.P.s of choice (any) is a choose-2 across Weapon Proficiencies. Additional Skills (one at levels 2, 5, 7, 10 and 13 from Electrical, Mechanical and Technical, each +10%) are a related-skill schedule with a count of 0 at creation; the book limits the Technical picks to skills about building, excavation, making things or working with machines and materials, which is not enforced. Swimming 88% and Climbing 76%/56% are printed under Natural Abilities at fixed figures and are kept as ability text, not skill grants; Prowl 64% is outside-the-shell only. || MAGIC: Frequency Jamming, Mend the Broken, Mystic Fulcrum and Negate Mechanics granted by name (the general invocations, not the warlock rows); one level one or two invocation at levels 3, 6, 9 and 12 as a schedule. || PSIONICS: the three named powers granted (catalog names Object Read (Psychometry) and Telemechanic Mental Operation); one Physical power at level 1 as powers_starting and at 5, 8, 11 and 15 as a schedule. The entry prints no minor/major/master tier; type major is a JUDGEMENT made at import because a psionics block shown without a tier reads as undefined in the picker and earns no psionics tag (four powers at level one, two of them Super, on M.E. x3 I.S.P.; the neighbouring Ichthylean entry with a similar grant prints Considered Major Psychics). || O.C.C.s: the entry has no O.C.C. line; nothing stored, stated in restrictions. || TECHNO-SHELL: the building rule (1D4+1 weapons and 1D4+4 other features at level one, one more per level from 2) and all nineteen features are special_abilities prose, one entry per feature, not pick groups; the picks are tracked by hand. || NOT STORED: starting money and equipment (none printed), body weight 100-150 lbs, height 5-6 feet, life span 60-90 years, NPC level 1D4+3, habitat, allies, enemies and slave value (GM Notes)."
---

## Lore

Picture a hermit crab the size of a person, carrying on its back a towering shell it has welded together out of scrap plate, machine parts, old tires, anchors, chain and anything else that caught its eye. That is a Junk Crab: a D-Bee from another dimension with ordinary human intelligence and an extraordinary feel for machines. They have four legs, two small three-digit hands, a long bendable neck, eyes on stalks, a pair of long feelers and drooping tendrils over a small beak that look very much like a mustache.

Junk Crabs live to salvage, build, tinker and repair. They patch hulls and armor, keep boats and simple machines running, and put up strange sculptures and underwater towers that go nowhere. Their taste is terrible and the newest technology defeats them, as do Techno-Wizard devices, but almost anything else they can get their claws on they can fix. Some anchor barges with a beacon repeating that they do repairs for free; in practice they expect a trade, though a scrap of metal, a spent E-Clip or a promise for next time usually satisfies them.

They are shy with strangers and warm up fast, behaving more like excitable children than adults: blunt, curious, easily bored, with no notion of manners, law, politics or money, but a firm sense of right and wrong. The shell is home, toolbox, armory and courage all at once. Inside it a Junk Crab is cheerful and confident; outside it, timid and unwilling to work.

## GM Notes

Also called Crab People, Mechanic Crabs and Salvage Crabs. The body alone stands 5-6 feet (1.5 to 1.8 m) and weighs 100-150 lbs (45 to 67.5 kg). Average life span 60-90 years. NPCs are level 1D4+3 or as the G.M. sets.

Habitat: met alone, in pairs or in groups of 3D4 in every ocean. Nobody knows where they really live; Lemurians have found the odd village of 1D6x10, and the race seems to be nomadic.

Allies: anyone who will trade junk or salvage or share technology. They will do simple repairs just for a look around a submarine''s engine room or at an alien device, and will often help someone in trouble on credit.

Enemies: pirates, demons, the undead and the supernatural. Rumor has it that Metztla hunt and eat them.

Value: worthless as slaves, since they give up and die in captivity. Treated as partners or left to run their own business they make good allies and capable traders, and many set up amphibious repair shops near islands, coasts, Dead Pools or ship graveyards.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'junk-crab');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'junk-crab';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-junk-crab-class.sql');
